WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:22.440
THE STATE OF TWITCH:
Twitch used to be about this.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.630
Now, it’s about this.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:30.310
Female gamers in sports bras, ASMR streams
that are actually about the visuals, and who

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:33.170
could forget the infamous hot tub streamers.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:38.170
At one point in time, Twitch was fighting
back on this type of content pretty hard.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:43.010
They would perma ban streamers for the slightest
of offenses, shadow ban their content, and/or

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.920
disable advertising for such channels.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.129
But, over the years, Twitch has become more
and more liberal.

00:00:49.129 --> 00:00:53.550
In fact, they’ve actually turned to embracing
more mature content.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:58.489
For example, two years ago, they added the
“hot tub” category into Twitch.

00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:02.660
And more recently, they have even gone as
far as unbanning streamers who were known

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.769
for pushing the boundaries like Indiefoxx.

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:11.189
I mean, her channel name itself has two xx
in it, surely, that’s just a coincidence.

00:01:11.189 --> 00:01:15.180
It’s not really surprising why so many streamers
have turned to this content.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.150
The bottom line is that it’s simply extraordinarily
lucrative.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:21.079
Take streamer Corinna Kopf for example.

00:01:21.079 --> 00:01:26.020
She’s one of the biggest names when it comes
to streamers with an OF, and when she switched

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:30.500
to OF, she made $4 million in the first month
and a million the next month.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:34.430
According to another streamer named Alinity,
when she transitioned to OF, she made more

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:37.800
in a month than she would’ve made on Twitch
in 10 years.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.110
That’s not the say that they were making
a small amount on Twitch itself though.

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:46.729
And given that Twitch get’s 50% of the revenue
that streamers pull in through the platform,

00:01:46.729 --> 00:01:51.329
it’s no wonder why they’ve turned to promoting
this content, but at what cost.

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:56.299
There’s no denying that mature content is
very much taboo even in western societies.

00:01:56.299 --> 00:02:01.020
And how many parents would want their kids
watching content on a platform that promotes

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.020
this.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:03.590
Safe to say that it’s not that many.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:07.420
It’s also likely that streamers will have
a lot more hesitancy when it comes to using

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:08.420
the platform.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:13.450
If Twitch becomes known for its mature content,
and you simply want to stream gaming content,

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:16.940
it’s more than likely that you choose to
stream on YouTube instead.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:22.470
So, it looks like Twitch may be digging their
own grave when it comes to the general audience,

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:24.440
but how did things get so bad.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:28.569
I mean, there’s plenty of creators who do
the same thing YouTube as well.

00:02:28.569 --> 00:02:34.021
Likely the most notable example is Belle Delphine,
but YouTube is by no means known for this

00:02:34.021 --> 00:02:35.021
content.

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:40.000
So, here’s Twitch devolution from gaming
and reaction content to not safe for work

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.000
content.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:46.490
THE ECONOMICS OF TWITCH:
Taking a look back, the downfall of Twitch

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:51.519
can actually be traced back to their more
recent surge which was actually 3 years ago

00:02:51.519 --> 00:02:54.040
at this point right at the beginning of the
pandemic.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:58.379
In April of 2020, Twitch’s streamers and
viewership basically doubled.

00:02:58.379 --> 00:03:02.849
Concurrent streams went from just over 50,000
to 100,000, and concurrent viewership went

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:05.860
from 1.4 million to 2.4 million.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:10.720
This momentum carried into early 2021, but
by the summer, Twitch was starting to show

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.680
the first signs of plateauing.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:16.580
And now that we’re two years out, this trend
is even clearer.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:20.209
From a normal person perspective, this is
by no means a problem.

00:03:20.209 --> 00:03:24.240
Twitch had simply reached it’s pinnacle
and it was starting to stabilize like any

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:25.280
mature company.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:29.190
But, from Amazon’s perspective, this was
a nightmare.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:33.510
If you didn’t know, Amazon is one of the
most infamous growth companies of all time.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:39.370
In the past 13 years, they haven’t even
had a single quarter of negative year of year

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:40.370
growth.

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:43.220
But, to achieve this, they’ve made some
pretty massive bets.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.980
In fact, they’ve basically sacrificed all
trace of profitability.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:51.889
They just reinvest everything they make and
if they still have profit left over, they’ll

00:03:51.889 --> 00:03:54.849
just keep prices the same until inflation
catches up.

00:03:54.849 --> 00:03:59.129
This is because the company strongly believes
that your margin is their opportunity.

00:03:59.129 --> 00:04:05.170
So, in theory, if they have no margin, no
one else will have any opportunity.

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:08.010
And they very much ran Twitch with this same
mindset as well.

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:14.430
Despite being a ginormous platform, it’s
not clear if Twitch is or ever was profitable.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:19.459
After all, it’s not cheap to stream and
store hundreds of millions of hours of content.

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:22.930
This is the main reason that Twitch doesn’t
really support 4K streaming.

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:29.060
It’s estimated that in 2019, it cost Amazon
$1.8 billion to run Twitch, but they only

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:31.729
pulled in $1.54 billion worth of revenue.

00:04:31.729 --> 00:04:37.790
The pandemic boom may have pushed Twitch into
profitability but only slightly if at all.

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:42.660
And the problem is that these wonky economics
only work if the company is constantly growing

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:45.660
at 20, 30, 40% per year.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:51.100
Stagnation is seen as a catastrophe and Twtich
hasn’t just been stagnating, they’ve actually

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:52.100
been declining.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:57.910
In fact, in November of 2022, Twitch had it’s
largest viewership drop in years of 10%.

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:02.639
Again, this isn’t to say that Twitch is
dying, it was just cooling off from the pandemic

00:05:02.639 --> 00:05:04.990
boom as people started doing other stuff again.

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:10.770
But, I think you can imagine the disgruntled
faces of Amazon executives after seeing this

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:11.770
performance.

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:12.770
Why wasn’t Twitch growing?

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:17.190
TikTok was growing, Instagram was growing,
and even YouTube which had been out for way

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:19.680
longer was growing.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.330
What was Twitch doing wrong?

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:25.720
Well, the answer was that they simply appealed
to a much more niche demographic due to their

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:26.720
business model.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:31.229
Unlike most popular services today, Twitch
actually makes most of their money from users

00:05:31.229 --> 00:05:33.210
directly paying creators.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:36.310
And there’s very few niches in which this
sort of strategy works.

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:41.580
So, by nature, Twitch is limited in how much
they can grow and they were left with two

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:42.580
options.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:45.789
Either, they could focus on promoting free
content and appealing to a broader audience

00:05:45.789 --> 00:05:47.660
or they could cut costs.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:52.290
Let’s just say, Twitch would aggressively
choose the latter.

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:57.520
BETTING AGAINST CREATORS:
Usually, platforms like YouTube are quite

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:02.110
generous when it comes to revenue sharing
with creators and the reasoning isn’t rocket

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:03.110
science.

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:06.970
The more that creators make, the more that
these platforms make, so it’s in their best

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:10.090
interest to align incentives and lock in creators.

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:14.630
This is why YouTube has been handing out exclusivity
deals left and right and why Spotify paid

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:17.910
Joe Rogan $200 million to switch to Spotify.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:23.509
But for a few years now, Twitch has been doing
the exact opposite and the creators who gave

00:06:23.509 --> 00:06:28.590
Twitch the confidence to do this were none
other than Ninja and Shroud, two of the platform’s

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:29.889
most popular streamers.

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:35.400
You see, back in late 2019, Microsoft paid
these creators a stupid amount of money to

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.919
switch to their streaming platform, Mixer.

00:06:37.919 --> 00:06:41.220
They paid Ninja $50 million and Shroud $10
million.

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:44.840
At first, this was seen as a massive loss
for Twitch.

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:48.370
Microsoft had literally bought out two of
their biggest creators.

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:53.050
But, to most people’s surprise, Twitch’s
viewership didn’t really go down after these

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:54.050
creators left.

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:58.160
And to make things even better for Twitch,
Microsoft would actually shut down Mixer in

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:02.050
mid 2020 and both streamers would return to
Twitch for free.

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:04.440
This was Twitch’s light bulb moment.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:07.940
The bottom line was that these creators had
no where else to go.

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:11.860
Sure, there are some exceptions like Ludwig
who have been able to successfully transition

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:16.530
to YouTube, but for the most part, streamers
only have one option: Twitch.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:21.460
And according to former Twitch employees,
the company would very much leverage this.

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:26.360
They would renew contracts with top streamers
with less favorable conditions and they were

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:30.120
even rooting for top streamers to leave as
it gave up and coming streamers more room

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:31.120
to grow.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:35.870
Apparently, Ninja leaving gave room for 5
to 6 new streamers to take his place.

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:41.280
This was no doubt a new strategy for streaming
platforms, but it was by no means a new strategy

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:42.280
for Amazon.

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:46.669
I’m sure you’re all familiar with the
terrible working conditions of Amazon workers.

00:07:46.669 --> 00:07:50.770
You’ve probably wondered why Amazon doesn’t
just fix these issues to improve employee

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:52.479
satisfaction and retention.

00:07:52.479 --> 00:07:56.580
Well, the answer is that they literally want
employees to leave.

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:57.580
They want them to constantly churn.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:07:58.580
Here’s the idea behind this.

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:01.650
Whenever you get a new job, you’ll probably
work your ass off to build up a good reputation.

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:05.830
But, as you become more tenured, you’ll
likely become more comfortable and settle

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:08.470
into a more normal working schedule.

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:14.000
Jeff Bezos sees this evolution as a march
to mediocrity, so Amazon tries to avoid this

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:15.289
at all costs.

00:08:15.289 --> 00:08:19.620
Instead, they want people to join Amazon,
work a crap ton, see that no one recognizes

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:21.330
their efforts, and then leave.

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:25.580
This way, they can repeat this process with
the next sucker in line.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:30.350
There’s no doubt that this is a toxic strategy,
but it’s worked for them quite well over

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:34.450
the past two decades and now they’re applying
the same strategy to Twitch.

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:39.700
Hungry creators jump onto the platform and
grind away at content hoping that just maybe

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:40.700
they can make it.

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:44.800
Once they make it though, they realize that
Twitch isn’t actually all that friendly

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:48.490
and they either get burnt out or switch to
YouTube.

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:52.220
This doesn’t really matter for Twitch though
as there’s hundreds of thousands of creators

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:54.700
who would be happy to take their place.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:59.339
While effective, this has made Twitch into
the same meat grinder as Amazon.

00:08:59.339 --> 00:09:04.779
TURNING TO NSFW CONTENT:
All of these cuts on the creator side worked

00:09:04.779 --> 00:09:06.070
out quite well.

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:10.720
In fact, Twitch is still actively cutting
perks for creators.

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:17.030
In late 2022, for example, Twitch nerfed creators
with a 70/30 revenue split down to 50/50 after

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:18.529
the first $100,000.

00:09:18.529 --> 00:09:23.160
This was an espeically savage move because
streamers were actually asking for all creators

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:28.430
to be boosted to a 70/30 model, but Twitch
went ahead and did the exact opposite.

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:33.490
But, while all of these moves made Twitch
less of a money pit, it didn’t solve Twitch’s

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:36.529
original problem which was stagnating growth.

00:09:36.529 --> 00:09:41.260
What they needed was new creator generated
content that appealed to a larger demographic

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:44.110
that people were willing to pay for directly.

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:49.010
There was really only one genre that accomplished
this and that’s of course simp content.

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:53.380
To be honest, it’s not surprising why this
content would perform so well on Twitch.

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:57.720
The platform is literally filled with decently
well off anti social gamers.

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:01.529
So, it’s no wonder why these people ate
up thirst traps and e-thots.

00:10:01.529 --> 00:10:02.529
But, as we discussed, Twitch was actually
resisting this content for the longest time.

00:10:02.529 --> 00:10:06.570
It’s likely that they didn’t want to associate
with this content or have it tarnish the brand

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:07.890
of Twitch or Amazon.

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:12.430
But, it seems like all of their worries flurried
away when they saw that other platforms were

00:10:12.430 --> 00:10:15.630
doing the same thing particularly Instagram
and TikTok.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:19.690
I mean, half the content on TikTok is crap
like this.

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:24.360
And as for Instagram, let’s be honest, ain't
no one following fitness models because they

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:26.260
inspire them to workout or something.

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:30.090
Even YouTube has turned to this type of content
when it comes to shorts.

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:34.970
So, somewhere along the line, Twitch figured,
if everyone else is doing it, why shouldn’t

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:35.970
we?

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:40.190
We should also mention that another risque
website has blown up over the past couple

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:41.190
of years.

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:45.300
The success of this platform likely just motivated
Twitch even more to make the switch.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:50.450
And judging by the content on Twitch today,
it looks like they’ve definitely made the

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:51.450
switch.

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:56.529
THE FUTURE OF TWITCH:
Looking forward, it doesn’t seem like things

00:10:56.529 --> 00:10:57.840
will get any better.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:03.190
Just one month ago, Twitch’s co founder
Emmett Shear stepped down as CEO, so now it’s

00:11:03.190 --> 00:11:07.240
truly just run by a bunch of Amazonions who
are looking for maximum growth and don’t

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:09.350
really care about the soul of the platform.

00:11:09.350 --> 00:11:13.709
So, you can bet that Twitch’s creators policies
will just get even less friendly, they’ll

00:11:13.709 --> 00:11:17.870
become even more of a meat grinder, and they’re
gonna embrace any avenue that allows them

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:19.149
to continue growing.

00:11:19.149 --> 00:11:24.209
Of course, I don’t think they’ll turn
to full on adult content, but with that being

00:11:24.209 --> 00:11:28.389
said, I do believe that they’ll continue
to push the boundaries in terms of what’s

00:11:28.389 --> 00:11:30.240
acceptable for a mainstream platform.

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:34.570
And given that TikTok and Instagram are very
much willing to do the same thing, Twitch’s

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:40.350
days as the gaming platform may very well
be coming to an end and that’s the sad state

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:41.360
of Twitch.

